(*
# Dump NPCs
`map/zips/npcs_helper.zip`: one compact json document per NPC definition, named
`<id>.json`, 16292 of them on cache 2625. Ports `SimbaNPCDumper.export` plus
`NpcExporter.simbaExport`.

## This is the *helper*, not `map/npcs.zip`

Two different files, and only one of them can be produced from a cache.
`map/npcs.zip` is what WaspLib actually reads, and building it needs NPC spawn
coordinates, which are not in the cache at all - `wasp-assets/tools/update_npcs.simba`
downloads them from `mejrs/data_osrs`. What that script *does* get from here is
every non-positional field: it opens `npcs_helper.zip`, looks the spawn's id up
as `<id>.json`, and copies `name`, `level`, `category`, `minimapdot`, `actions`,
`size` and `colors` across verbatim (`update_npcs.simba:90,215-233`).

So this unit's contract is the seven keys, and nothing else.

## The shape, which is literal

`NpcExporter.simbaExport` builds a `JsonObject` by hand, so the key order is
whatever the source says and there is nothing clever to reproduce:

```java
name, level, category, minimapdot, actions, size, colors
```

- `name` <- `npc.name`, which is the string `"null"` and not a json null when the
  definition never set it.
- `level` <- `npc.combatLevel`. Declared `-1` in `NpcDefinition.java:61`, but no
  definition in cache 2625 leaves it there: 0 is the lowest value in the archive
  and in the committed zip alike.
- `category` <- `npc.category`.
- `minimapdot` <- `npc.isMinimapVisible`, a json boolean - `true` / `false`,
  lowercase.
- `actions` <- the op slots, **compacted**. Java grows `ops` to fit the highest
  index it sets and leaves `null` in the gaps, then skips those on the way out
  (`NpcExporter.java:76-78`), so a definition with slots 0, 2 and 3 filled
  writes a three-element array and the indices are gone. 5993 of the 16292
  definitions have such a gap; NPC 0, the Tool Leprechaun, is the first.
- `size` <- `[npc.size, npc.size, height]`. Both of the first two really do come
  from the same scalar field - there is no separate Y size.
- `colors` <- {ref}`TModelStats.OfModels`, order and repeats included.

**No filtering.** Every file of the CONFIGS/NPC archive is loaded and exported,
which is what makes the entry count exactly the archive's file count.

## Escaping: the plain form, not the item dump's

`simbaExport` returns `obj.toString()`, and `JsonElement.toString()` builds a
bare `JsonWriter` whose `htmlSafe` is **false**. `ItemExporter` goes through
`GsonBuilder`, whose default is **true**. So this file uses {ref}`JSONEscape`
and `jsons/item-definitions.zip` uses {ref}`JSONEscapeHTML`, and swapping them
is a silent parity loss rather than a crash.

Measured on the committed zip: 297 entries carry `<col=..>` markup and 206
carry an apostrophe, all raw, and there is not one `\uXXXX` escape anywhere in
the 16292 files. Not one `"` or `\` either, so the escapes the two forms agree
on are unexercised here - the only escaping this artifact ever performs is the
escaping it must *not* do.

Non-ASCII passes through as raw UTF-8: NPC 5521 is `Le-sabr<C3><A8>`.

## One divergence the loader cannot avoid

Java distinguishes "op slot never set" (`null`) from "op slot set to the empty
string", and would emit the latter as `""`. {ref}`TEntityOps` stores both as
`''`, so this writer skips both. Unobservable on real data - no entry in the
committed zip has an empty action - and it would take a cache revision that
stores a zero-length op string to make it observable.

## Entry order is ours to choose, because the committed one is not reproducible

CI ran `cd .../NPCs && zip -r npcs-helper.zip .`, and `zip -r` on a directory
walks in **readdir order**. The committed archive starts `9929.json`,
`11762.json`, `5764.json` - ext4 hash order, not sorted, not numeric, and not a
function of anything in the cache. It is also not `HashMap` bucket order, which
was checked: `values()` over int keys below 32768 comes out ascending, and the
committed file is not ascending.

Nothing reads it, either - `update_npcs.simba` opens entries by name. So
{ref}`DumpEntryOrder` emits byte-lexicographic order, matching
`DumpItemDefinitions` and matching what the shell glob `zip -r x.zip ./*.json`
would have produced.

## Verification, and the revision this settles
Byte-for-byte against every one of the 16292 committed entries: **16292/16292
on cache 2625, and 16286/16292 on 2639**. See `dev/tests/412_npchelper.simba`.

So the committed `npcs_helper.zip` **is** from cache 2625, which nothing outside
the data said - the file is absent from `git show --stat 7b40e8c`, the commit
that named the revision. The six entries that separate the two are the
Gauntlet's `Crystalline`/`Corrupted` `Rat`/`Unicorn`/`Wolf` (9026, 9029, 9031,
9040, 9043, 9045), each differing in `level` alone: 24->23, 48->45, 74->69,
34->33, 64->61, 102->97, a combat-level rebalance between the revisions.

Note what that corrects and what it does not. `TModelStats.OfModels` really is
byte-identical across the two revisions for all 16292 definitions
(`dev/tests/410_modelstats.simba`) - the six drifted entries have unchanged
`size` and `colors`, which 412 re-checks. `combatLevel` is a definition field,
not a model one, so it was outside that measurement.

The zip's own bytes do **not** match the committed container and are not meant
to: {ref}`TZipWriter` deflates harder than the JDK's and pins its own
timestamps. Verification is per entry, never whole-file CRC32.
*)
{$DEFINE WL_CACHE_DUMP_NPCS_INCLUDED}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DUMP_COMMON_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE dumpcommon.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DUMP_ZIPWRITER_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE zipwriter.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DUMP_MODELSTATS_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE modelstats.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_TYPES_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../cachetypes.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_STORE_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../store.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DEF_NPC_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../defs/npc.simba}{$ENDIF}

// Gson writes a json boolean, which is lowercase. Lape's ToStr(Boolean) is
// 'True' / 'False', so it cannot be used here.
function _NpcJsonBool(b: Boolean): String;
begin
  if b then Result := 'true' else Result := 'false';
end;

(*
## NpcHelperJSON
```pascal
function NpcHelperJSON(npc: TNpcDefinition; height: Integer; colors: TIntegerArray): String;
```
One helper document, exactly as `NpcExporter.simbaExport(height, colors)` writes
it: compact, no HTML escaping, no trailing newline.

`height` and `colors` are the entity's model statistics -
{ref}`TModelStats.OfModels` computes both. They are parameters rather than being
derived here for the same reason the Java passes them in: the loop that produces
them needs the store, and this function needs nothing but the definition, which
is what makes it testable on a definition alone.

Every number goes out as an integer. There is no floating-point value anywhere
in this artifact and there is no path by which one could appear: `level`,
`category`, `size` and `height` are `Integer` fields and `colors` is a packed
RGB, so `ToStr` is safe on all of them.

Example:
```pascal
WriteLn NpcHelperJSON(npc, 0, []);
// {"name":"null","level":0,"category":1458,"minimapdot":true,"actions":[],"size":[1,1,0],"colors":[]}
```
*)
function NpcHelperJSON(npc: TNpcDefinition; height: Integer; colors: TIntegerArray): String;
var
  i, written: Integer;
begin
  Result := '{"name":"' + JSONEscape(npc.Name) + '"' +
            ',"level":' + ToStr(npc.CombatLevel) +
            ',"category":' + ToStr(npc.Category) +
            ',"minimapdot":' + _NpcJsonBool(npc.IsMinimapVisible) +
            ',"actions":[';

  // `if (get(i) != null) actions.add(...)`. An unset slot is '' here, so the
  // separator has to count what was written, not the loop index.
  for i := 0 to High(npc.Ops.Ops) do
  begin
    if npc.Ops.Ops[i] = '' then Continue;
    if written > 0 then Result += ',';
    Result += '"' + JSONEscape(npc.Ops.Ops[i]) + '"';
    Inc(written);
  end;

  // size.add(npc.size); size.add(npc.size); size.add(height);
  Result += '],"size":[' + ToStr(npc.Size) + ',' + ToStr(npc.Size) + ',' +
            ToStr(height) + '],"colors":[';

  for i := 0 to High(colors) do
  begin
    if i > 0 then Result += ',';
    Result += ToStr(colors[i]);
  end;

  Result += ']}';
end;

(*
## NpcHelperJSONOf
```pascal
function NpcHelperJSONOf(var store: TCacheStore; textures: array of TTextureDefinition; id: Integer; data: TByteArray): String;
```
{ref}`NpcHelperJSON` for one raw CONFIGS/NPC file: decode it, compute its model
statistics against `store`, serialise.

`data` is one element of `store.ReadFiles(2, Ord(EConfigType.NPC), [])` and `id`
is the matching entry of the reference table's `FileIds`.

`textures` is {ref}`ModelStatsTextures` for the same store. It is a parameter
because it is the one thing here that is worth loading once: rebuilding it per
definition would decompress the TEXTURES archive 16292 times.

There is **no memoisation**, per definition or per model, matching
`SimbaNPCDumper` - see the note in `modelstats.simba` on why a memo here is the
one optimisation worth refusing.
*)
function NpcHelperJSONOf(var store: TCacheStore; textures: array of TTextureDefinition; id: Integer; data: TByteArray): String;
var
  npc: TNpcDefinition;
  stats: TModelStats;
begin
  npc := TNpcDefinition.Load(id, data);
  stats := TModelStats.OfModels(store, textures, npc.Models);
  Result := NpcHelperJSON(npc, stats.Height, stats.Colors);
end;

// A String as its raw bytes. `Result := []` first because SetLength keeps the
// overlapping prefix of whatever the slot held before.
function _NpcHelperBytes(s: String): TByteArray;
begin
  Result := [];
  if s = '' then Exit;
  SetLength(Result, Length(s));
  Move(s[1], Result[0], Length(s));
end;


(*
## DumpNPCHelper
```pascal
procedure DumpNPCHelper(var store: TCacheStore; zipPath: String);
```
Writes the whole `map/zips/npcs_helper.zip` for `store`: every file of the
CONFIGS/NPC archive, unfiltered, named `<id>.json`, in byte-lexicographic entry
order.

This is the slow half of the NPC pipeline - it decodes every model every NPC
points at, ~30 s over the 16292 definitions on this machine. Nothing here is
cached between entities on purpose; see `modelstats.simba`.

```pascal
store := TCacheStore.Create(cacheDir);
DumpNPCHelper(store, dump.Path('map/zips/npcs_helper.zip'));
```
*)
procedure DumpNPCHelper(var store: TCacheStore; zipPath: String);
var
  files: array of TByteArray;
  textures: array of TTextureDefinition;
  table: TCacheRefTable;
  ids, order: TIntegerArray;
  names: TStringArray;
  zip: TZipWriter;
  a, i, k: Integer;
begin
  table := store.GetRefTable(Ord(EIndexType.CONFIGS));
  a := table.IndexOfArchive(Ord(EConfigType.NPC));
  if a = -1 then
    raise 'DumpNPCs: this cache has no CONFIGS/NPC archive';

  ids := table.Archives[a].FileIds;
  files := store.ReadFiles(Ord(EIndexType.CONFIGS), Ord(EConfigType.NPC), []);
  if Length(files) <> Length(ids) then
    raise 'DumpNPCs: the NPC archive holds ' + ToStr(Length(files)) +
          ' files but its reference table names ' + ToStr(Length(ids));

  textures := ModelStatsTextures(store);

  SetLength(names, Length(ids));
  for i := 0 to High(ids) do
    names[i] := ToStr(ids[i]) + '.json';
  order := DumpEntryOrder(names);

  zip.Init();
  for i := 0 to High(order) do
  begin
    if i mod 1000 = 0 then
      WriteLn('DumpNPCs: helper ', i, '/', Length(order));
    k := order[i];
    zip.AddBytes(names[k], _NpcHelperBytes(NpcHelperJSONOf(store, textures, ids[k], files[k])));
  end;

  zip.Save(zipPath);
end;
